Dedicated to Anna Grigoryevna Dostoevskaya
§
Truly, truly, I tell you: unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains a single grain. But if it dies, it bears much fruit.
The Gospel of John, chapter 12, verse 24.
§
From the Author
§
As I begin the life story of my hero, Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov, I find myself somewhat at a loss. Although I call Alexey Fyodorovitch my hero, I know perfectly well that he is by no means a great man. So I foresee the inevitable questions: What is so remarkable about your Alexey Fyodorovitch that you have chosen him as your hero? What has he done? Who knows him, and what is he known for? Why should I, the reader, spend time learning the facts of his life?
§
The last question is the most dangerous, since I can answer it only by saying, “Perhaps you will see for yourself in the novel.” But what if people read the novel and do not see it, if they do not agree that my Alexey Fyodorovitch is remarkable? I ask because, to my regret, I foresee precisely that. He is remarkable to me, but I very much doubt whether I can prove it to the reader. The trouble is that he may be a man of action, but the nature of his action remains uncertain, not yet clear. Still, it would be strange to demand clarity from people in a time like ours. One thing, perhaps, is fairly certain: he is a strange man, even an eccentric. But strangeness and eccentricity are more a disadvantage than a claim to attention, especially when everyone is trying to bring particulars together and find some common sense in the general confusion. An eccentric, in most cases, represents precisely the particular and the isolated. Is that not so?
§
If you disagree with this last proposition and answer, “No,” or “Not always,” I may take heart about the significance of my hero, Alexey Fyodorovitch. For it is not merely that an eccentric is “not always” something particular and isolated. On the contrary, sometimes it is he who carries within him the very heart of the whole, while all the other people of his time have, for some reason, been torn away from him for a while, as though by a passing gust of wind …
§
I would not have gone into these uninteresting and obscure explanations, but simply begun without a preface: if people like the book, they will read it anyway. The difficulty, however, is that I have one life story but two novels. The principal novel is the second. It concerns my hero's life and actions in our own time, at the very moment in which we are now living. The first novel, on the other hand, takes place thirteen years ago and is hardly even a novel, only a single moment in my hero's early youth. I cannot do without this first novel, because much in the second would otherwise be incomprehensible. But that only makes my original difficulty greater: if even I, the biographer himself, believe that perhaps one novel would already be too much for such a modest and undefined hero, how can I come forward with two? How am I to explain such presumption on my part?
§
Unable to find answers to these questions, I have decided to pass them by without answering them at all. Of course, the perceptive reader guessed long ago that this was where I was heading from the start, and has merely been annoyed at my wasting empty words and precious time. Here I can give a precise answer: I wasted empty words and precious time, first out of courtesy and second out of cunning. At least, I can say, I gave some warning beforehand. In fact, I am even glad that my novel has divided of its own accord into two narratives, “while retaining the essential unity of the whole.” After getting to know the first narrative, the reader can decide whether the second is worth taking up. Naturally, no one is under any obligation. You can put the book down after two pages of the first narrative and never open it again. But there are considerate readers who will insist on finishing it in order to make sure their impartial judgment is sound. All Russian critics, for example, are like that. Where they are concerned, I can now feel easier: despite all their care and conscientiousness, I am giving them the most legitimate excuse to abandon the narrative after the novel's first episode. That is the whole preface. I entirely agree that it is unnecessary, but since it has been written, let it stay.
§
And now to the matter at hand.
